# Section 2.3: The Compilation Pipeline

 
 ⚙️ Build Process 
 ⏱️ Estimated Time: 25 minutes 
 

---

 
 
 
 ✦ The C# Assembly Line: How Code Becomes Action 
 The C# compilation pipeline is a multi-stage transformation. Your human-readable source code doesn't talk to the CPU directly; instead, it travels through a specialized assembly line that turns syntax into symbols , and symbols into cross-platform instructions that can run on any device. 
 
 
 
 
 
 

---

## 🔄 From Text to Running Program

When you write C# code, you're writing **human-readable text**. But your computer's CPU doesn't understand C#—it only understands machine code (binary instructions specific to your processor). The compilation pipeline bridges this gap.

 
 💡 
 
 The Key Insight 
 C# uses a two-stage compilation process. This is what makes .NET applications portable across different operating systems. 
 
 

---

## The Four Stages of the Pipeline

 
 ‹ 
 
 
 1. Raw Materials: Source Code 
 The journey begins with your .cs files. At this stage, the code is just text. The compiler performs Lexical Analysis , breaking your text into 'tokens' like keywords, names, and operators. 
 
 
 2. The Factory: Roslyn Compiler 
 Named after the 'Roslyn' platform, the C# compiler builds a complex digital map called an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) . It checks for grammar errors and ensures your logic follows C# rules. 
 
 
 3. Intermediate: MSIL Bytecode 
 Instead of making machine code for one CPU, Roslyn outputs Microsoft Intermediate Language (MSIL) . This is the 'glowing' secret to C#'s cross-platform power—one set of instructions for all OSs. 
 
 
 4. Finished Product: Native Code 
 When you click 'Run,' the JIT (Just-In-Time) Compiler takes the MSIL and translates it into the specific language of your hardware (Windows, Mac, or Linux) right before it executes. 
 
 
